  "body": "Spec says:\r\n```\r\n  [CEReactions] attribute unsigned long width;\r\n```\r\nand also:\r\n> The width and height attributes on embed, iframe, img, object or video elements, and input elements with a type attribute in the Image Button state and that either represents an image or that the user expects will eventually represent an image, map to the dimension properties 'width' and 'height' on the element respectively.\r\n\r\nThat means that if I have `<video width=\"50%\">` the following will be true, per spec:\r\n\r\n* `.width` on that video will return the integer `50`\r\n* The width of the video will be half the width of its parent element.\r\n\r\nwidth/height on `<video>` are the only things that do that.  Other dimension attributes either reflect as strings (most of them) or don't reflect  at all (width/height on `<img>`, which return the actual rendered width or intrinsic width).\r\n\r\nIt seems that Blink and WebKit follow the spec here; Gecko returns `0` in the above situation.  I'm probably just going to change Gecko to align with Blink and WebKit, but it's not clear that this is ideal behavior...",
